  <section class="fbgr-section fbgr-section--alt">
    <div class="fbgr-wrap">
      <h2 class="fbgr-h2">Five things worth keeping in front of you</h2>
      <p class="fbgr-sub">The takeaways from 92 minutes of FERS, in plain language.</p>
      <ol class="fbgr-list">
        <li>
          <span class="fbgr-num">1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Keep your own records</h3>
            <p>Download your eOPF &mdash; every SF-50 and your DD-214 &mdash; plus your Social Security statement. After you retire, HR can no longer pull these for you, and OPM uses them to calculate your years of creditable service.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="fbgr-num">2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Know when you can retire</h3>
            <p>A full, unreduced pension takes <em>both</em> your Minimum Retirement Age <em>and</em> the service: MRA with 30 years, age 60 with 20 years, or age 62 with at least 5 years. MRA + 10 is an option, but can reduce your pension by roughly 5% per year you are under 62.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="fbgr-num">3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>The pension formula is simpler than it looks</h3>
            <p>Years of creditable service &times; 1% &times; your high-3 average salary. That 1% becomes 1.1% if you retire at 62 or later with at least 20 years of service.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="fbgr-num">4</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Your unused sick leave counts</h3>
            <p>At retirement, unused sick leave converts into extra months of creditable service and increases your pension &mdash; a benefit you never paid a premium for.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="fbgr-num">5</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Protect your spouse</h3>
            <p>If you are married, electing a survivor benefit is the only way your spouse keeps your federal health insurance after you pass away. End of the month &mdash; often December 31 &mdash; can also help you make the most of your final benefits.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
